      "costUsd": 0.007993,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's how big this gets. Nine thousand two hundred dollars goes into a retirement fund at the end of every year, in a model with a fixed seven per cent a year. After forty years, that's one million, eight hundred and thirty-six thousand, six hundred and forty-three dollars, from just three hundred and sixty-eight thousand dollars of deposits. Start ten years later, and after thirty years it's eight hundred and sixty-nine thousand dollars. Those first ten deposits cost only ninety-two thousand dollars, but missing them costs nearly a million. They're the deposits that grow the longest."
